3.1 Authentication
==================

The command ‘spofy-authenticate’ initiates the Spotify OAuth2
authorization flow.  It starts a temporary HTTP server on
‘spofy-redirect-port’, opens the user's default browser to Spotify's
authorization page, and waits for the redirect callback.  Once the user
grants access, Spofy exchanges the authorization code for access and
refresh tokens, stores them in ‘spofy-token-file’, and shuts down the
temporary server.

   After successful authentication, Spofy runs
‘spofy-authenticated-hook’.  You only need to authenticate once;
subsequent sessions reuse the stored refresh token to obtain new access
tokens automatically.


File: spofy.info,  Node: Dashboard and global control,  Next: Transient popup,  Prev: Authentication,  Up: Commands

3.2 Dashboard and global control
================================

The command ‘spofy’ is the main entry point.  It ensures that the user
is authenticated (prompting if not), starts player state polling, and
opens the ‘*Spofy*’ dashboard buffer.  The dashboard displays three
sections:

  1. *Now playing:* the current track name, artist, album, a text
     progress bar, and playback state indicators (play/pause, shuffle,
     repeat).
  2. *Recently played:* the last 10 tracks, each a clickable button that
     replays the track.
  3. *Your playlists:* the user's first 10 playlists, each a clickable
     button that opens the playlist detail view.  A "View all
     playlists..." link at the bottom opens the full playlists list.

   The now-playing section refreshes automatically when the player state
changes.  The command ‘spofy-dashboard-refresh’ forces a full refresh of
the entire dashboard.

   The command ‘spofy-stop’ stops player state polling and disables
‘spofy-global-mode’.  Use it when you are done with Spofy and want to
stop all background activity.

   The dashboard keymap provides these bindings:

Key   Command
---------------------------
‘p’   ‘spofy-play-pause’
‘n’   ‘spofy-next’
‘b’   ‘spofy-previous’
   ‘/’ | ‘spofy-menu’ |
‘g’   ‘spofy-dashboard-refresh’
‘q’   ‘quit-window’


File: spofy.info,  Node: Transient popup,  Next: Global minor mode,  Prev: Dashboard and global control,  Up: Commands

3.3 Transient popup
===================

The command ‘spofy-menu’ opens a transient popup that provides quick
access to all Spofy commands from any buffer.  The popup header shows
the currently playing track.  When ‘spofy-global-mode’ is active, this
command is bound to ‘spofy-global-key’ (default ‘C-c s’).

   The popup is organized into three columns:

   • *Playback:* play/pause, next, previous, seek forward, seek
     backward.
   • *Browse:* search tracks/albums/artists/playlists, library,
     playlists list, device selection.
   • *Volume, Mode & Other:* volume controls (volume up/down keep the
     menu open), shuffle/repeat toggles, dashboard, quit.

   A sub-transient for the library (*note Library management::) is
available from the browse column.


File: spofy.info,  Node: Global minor mode,  Next: Playback control,  Prev: Transient popup,  Up: Commands

3.4 Global minor mode
=====================

The command ‘spofy-global-mode’ toggles a global minor mode.  When
enabled, it:

   • Binds ‘spofy-global-key’ to ‘spofy-menu’.
   • Starts player state polling.
   • Enables the mode-line display (if ‘spofy-enable-mode-line’ is
     non-nil).

   When disabled, it reverses all of these effects.  The ‘spofy’ command
enables this mode automatically.


File: spofy.info,  Node: Playback control,  Next: Polling,  Prev: Global minor mode,  Up: Commands

3.5 Playback control
====================

The command ‘spofy-play-pause’ toggles playback between playing and
paused.  It checks the current state and sends the appropriate API call.

   The commands ‘spofy-next’ and ‘spofy-previous’ skip to the next or
previous track, respectively.

   The commands ‘spofy-seek-forward’ and ‘spofy-seek-backward’ move the
playback position by ‘spofy-seek-seconds’ seconds (*note Playback
options: Playback.).

   The commands ‘spofy-volume-up’ and ‘spofy-volume-down’ adjust the
volume by ‘spofy-volume-step’ percent.  The command ‘spofy-volume-set’
prompts for an exact volume level between 0 and 100.

   The command ‘spofy-toggle-shuffle’ toggles shuffle mode on or off.
The command ‘spofy-toggle-repeat’ cycles through three repeat states:
off, context (repeat the current album/playlist), and track (repeat the
current track).

   The command ‘spofy-play-track’ plays a specific track by its Spotify
URI.  If an optional context URI is provided (an album or playlist URI),
playback starts within that context at the specified track.  The command
‘spofy-play-context’ plays an entire context (album, playlist, or
artist) from the beginning.  Both commands are used internally by the
browse and search interfaces.


File: spofy.info,  Node: Polling,  Next: Device management,  Prev: Playback control,  Up: Commands

3.6 Polling
===========

The command ‘spofy-player-start-polling’ starts a timer that polls the
Spotify player state at ‘spofy-poll-interval’ second intervals.  Polling
is necessary because the Spotify Web API does not support push
notifications.  When the player state changes, Spofy runs
‘spofy-player-state-changed-hook’.  When the track changes specifically,
it also runs ‘spofy-player-track-changed-hook’.  When the API reports no
active player session (e.g.  playback ended or the device went idle),
Spofy clears the player state and runs ‘spofy-player-state-changed-hook’
so that the mode line and tab bar reflect that nothing is playing.
Polling errors are caught unconditionally (even when ‘debug-on-error’ is
non-nil) to prevent them from stalling the repeat timer.

   The command ‘spofy-player-stop-polling’ cancels the polling timer.
Polling is started automatically by the ‘spofy’ command and
‘spofy-global-mode’, and stopped by ‘spofy-stop’.


File: spofy.info,  Node: Device management,  Next: Search,  Prev: Polling,  Up: Commands

3.7 Device management
=====================

The Spotify Web API can control playback on any device logged into the
user's account: desktop apps, mobile phones, web players, smart
speakers, and more.  The command ‘spofy-select-device’ fetches the list
of available devices, presents them in a ‘completing-read’ prompt, and
transfers playback to the selected device.

   When no active device is found, Spofy's behavior depends on
‘spofy-no-device-action’ (*note Playback options: Playback.).


File: spofy.info,  Node: Search,  Next: Browsing,  Prev: Device management,  Up: Commands

3.8 Search
==========

Spofy provides four search commands, each targeting a specific entity
type:

   • ‘spofy-search-tracks’ searches for matching tracks.
   • ‘spofy-search-albums’ searches for matching albums.
   • ‘spofy-search-artists’ searches for matching artists.
   • ‘spofy-search-playlists’ searches for matching playlists.

   When ‘spofy-consult’ is loaded, these commands use incremental search
via ‘consult--dynamic-collection’: the minibuffer appears immediately
and candidates update live as you type.  Without ‘spofy-consult’, they
fall back to prompting for a query string and displaying results in a
tabulated list.

   Each command creates a ‘*Spofy Search: TYPE*’ buffer using
‘tabulated-list-mode’.  The columns vary by entity type:

Type        Columns
---------------------------------------------------------------
Tracks      Play indicator, Name, Artist(s), Album, Duration
Albums      Name, Artist(s), Year, Tracks
Artists     Name, Genres, Followers
Playlists   Name, Owner, Tracks, Public

   If the currently playing track appears in the search results, it is
highlighted with the ‘spofy-playing’ face and prefixed with a ▶ icon.

* Menu:

* Navigating search results::


